There's a lot here, but first off, you can find the Capture File Properties in the bottom right on the little page icon. It's a bit less intimidating and shows you info about the file, including:
    name, length, hash, time, capture, interfaces, comments, etc.

Wireshark uses the OSI model for dissection, here's how:
    Double click a packet to view its details in the details pane
    when you do that, the corresponding he will highlight in the bytes pane
    You can see the frame, ehernet protocol, TCP, HTTP, etc in the details pane

To search for specific packets you need to know the type of information in the packet. This includes four types of inputs: Display filter, Hex, String, and Regex. You must ensure you're searching in the correct pane, as a search that would be viable in details will return "nothing found" in the hex pane.

Wireshark can intercept and discover files transferred through the wire for selected protocol streams: DICOM, HTTP, IMF, SMB, and TFTP.